      "final_chatbot_response": "In this module, the main difference between primary and secondary research lies in the source of the data being used. \n\nPrimary research involves collecting new data specifically for the current project. This can include methods such as surveys, interviews, observations, or experiments. On the other hand, secondary research involves analyzing information that already exists, such as journal articles, reports, datasets, case studies, and official statistics.\n\nPrimary research is tailored to the research question and context but may require more time, access, skills, and ethical planning. Secondary research is usually faster and provides broader existing knowledge, but the data may have been collected for another purpose or context.\n\nIt is common for research projects to use both primary and secondary research methods to strengthen the project when their roles are clearly defined. For example, a learner might use published studies for general understanding and a small customer survey to explore specific attitudes relevant to the assignment context.\n\nBoth primary and secondary research have their strengths and limitations, and the choice between them should be based on the research question, purpose, practical considerations, and methodological trade-offs.",
