      "final_chatbot_response": "Based on the course material chunks provided, here are some key points to consider when reviewing your methodology:\n\n1. **Consolidate Methodology Decisions**: Ensure that your methodology aligns with your research question, evidence needs, feasibility, ethics, and planned analysis. Your methodology section should form a coherent chain from the research question to data collection and analysis.\n\n2. **Consider Alternative Research Methodologies**: Reflect on credible alternative approaches that could have been used instead of your chosen methodology. Compare the additional or different evidence that alternatives might provide, along with their costs and limitations. Fairly compare alternatives, explaining potential benefits and trade-offs without automatically assuming an alternative would be better.\n\n3. **Evaluate Your Research Methods**: Assess whether your chosen methods have achieved the research objectives. Evaluate the strengths, weaknesses, practical challenges, bias, reliability, validity, or credibility of your methodology. Discuss whether another approach could have produced stronger evidence and evaluate methods against your objectives.\n\n4. **Confirm Evidence Analysis and Interpretation**: Make sure that your evidence has been analyzed and interpreted appropriately. Select suitable quantitative or qualitative techniques, produce clear summaries or themes, interpret findings against objectives and literature, consider triangulation where useful, and document limitations. Explain how each major finding was derived from evidence and what it does and does not support.\n\n5. **Choose and Justify Your Methodology**: Select your methodology based on the research question and practical constraints rather than personal preference. Justify your methodology choices by explaining why the chosen philosophy, approach, methods, sampling, data collection, and analysis are suitable while considering time, access, cost, ethics, strengths, and limitations. Discuss alternatives to demonstrate critical decision-making.\n\nWhen reviewing your methodology, ensure that it is coherent, aligned with your research objectives, and well-justified based on the considerations mentioned above. If you have specific questions or need further guidance on any aspect of your methodology, feel free to ask for more targeted assistance.",
